/// United States 401(k) retirement plans.
///
/// A 401(k) is a tax-deferred account. Pay that goes in as a pre-tax deferral
/// arrives with no basis, so all of it, and all of its growth, is taxable when
/// it comes out. This system caps what goes in each year, counts what comes
/// out as income, prices a withdrawal before age 59½, and warns when a
/// required distribution is missed.
///
/// The figures are community-maintained data. Verify them against the IRS
/// (irs.gov, the yearly "COLA" notice) before relying on them, and correct
/// them in a pull request when they are wrong.
system us/401k

use us


/// An employer-sponsored retirement plan (IRC §401(k)).
kind 401k : tax-deferred
  liquidity 5d
  has employer entity

  /// Elective deferrals are capped for each person and calendar year
  /// (IRC §402(g)), with a catch-up from age 50 and a larger one from 60
  /// through 63 (IRC §414(v)). This payment took the year's deferrals over the
  /// cap. Ask payroll to lower the deferral. Any excess must also be returned
  /// by the plan before April 15 of the next year, or it is taxed twice
  /// (IRC §402(g)(2)).
  law deferral-limit
    on in
    when from is wages
    let extra = if owner.age >= 60y and owner.age < 64y then super-catch-up[date] else if owner.age >= 50y then catch-up[date] else empty
    require total(in, year) <= limit[date] + extra "401(k) deferrals over the yearly limit"

  /// A deferral is pay you chose not to receive, so it is not taxed this year
  /// (IRC §402(e)(3)). The wages law counted it as pay; this law counts it as
  /// deferred and takes it back out of `agi`. Roth deferrals are not modelled.
  law pretax-deferral
    on in
    when from is wages
    count amount as pretax
    count -amount as agi

  /// Money that comes out of a 401(k) is ordinary income in the year it comes
  /// out (IRC §402(a)): deferrals and their growth were never taxed. It is
  /// counted as a distribution. A rollover into another tax-deferred account
  /// is not one.
  law count-distributions
    on gain
    when gain > empty
    when not to is tax-deferred
    count gain as distributions
    count gain as agi

  /// Money taken out before age 59½ carries an additional 10% tax on top of
  /// the income tax (IRC §72(t)). This withdrawal is priced at that 10%. If an
  /// exception applies (disability, separation from service at 55 or later,
  /// substantially equal payments, and others in §72(t)(2)), keep the
  /// withdrawal and mark it `!` with the reason.
  law early-withdrawal
    on gain
    when gain > empty
    when not to is tax-deferred
    require owner.age >= 59y6m else owe 10% * gain to irs as early-withdrawal-penalty "401(k) withdrawal before age 59½"

  /// From age 73, an owner must take a minimum distribution from a 401(k)
  /// every year (IRC §401(a)(9)). It is the balance at the end of the previous
  /// year divided by a divisor from the IRS Uniform Lifetime Table (Pub. 590-B),
  /// 26.5 at age 73. Nothing came out this year. Take the distribution before
  /// December 31, or the shortfall is taxed at 25% (IRC §4974). The first one
  /// may wait until April 1 of the following year. Owners born in 1960 or later
  /// start at 75.
  law required-minimum-distribution
    each year
    when owner.age >= 73y
    when self.balance > empty
    warn total(out, year) > empty "no required minimum distribution taken this year"


// The elective deferral limit (IRC §402(g)).
// Notice 2023-75 (2024), Notice 2024-80 (2025), Notice 2025-67 (2026).
param limit
  2024 23_000 USD
  2025 23_500 USD
  2026 24_500 USD

// The catch-up for age 50 and over (IRC §414(v)).
param catch-up
  2024 7_500 USD
  2025 7_500 USD
  2026 8_000 USD

// The larger catch-up for ages 60 through 63 (SECURE 2.0 §109), from 2025.
// The 2024 row repeats the ordinary catch-up: there was no larger one.
// verify: the 2026 figure against Notice 2025-67.
param super-catch-up
  2024  7_500 USD
  2025 11_250 USD
  2026 11_250 USD
